---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../components/Eyebrow.astro";
import DisplayHeading from "../components/DisplayHeading.astro";
import DarkCTA from "../components/DarkCTA.astro";
import GoogleReviewsBadge from "../components/GoogleReviewsBadge.astro";
import WhatsAppIcon from "../components/icons/WhatsAppIcon.astro";
import PhotoServiceCalculator from "../components/PhotoServiceCalculator.astro";
import { business } from "../data/business";

const description =
  "High-resolution artwork scanning in Singapore. Studio-grade flatbed digitisation with calibrated colour matching, ready for archival reprints and resale.";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  serviceType: "Art scanning and digitisation",
  provider: {
    "@type": "LocalBusiness",
    name: "Fine Art Printing",
    url: "https://fineartprinting.com.sg",
  },
  areaServed: { "@type": "Country", name: "Singapore" },
};

const ctaText = encodeURIComponent(
  "Hi Fine Art Printing, I'd like a quote for art scanning. I've attached photos of the artwork(s) and a short description.",
);
const waLink = `${business.whatsappLink}?text=${ctaText}`;

const whyScanWithUs = [
  {
    title: "Flatbed first, camera only when we have to",
    body:
      "We always prefer flatbed scanning for accuracy and detail. Photographing introduces lens distortion, lighting variance, and reflection. We only switch to camera capture when an artwork is too large to scan in pieces.",
  },
  {
    title: "Colour matching, best effort",
    body:
      "We work hard to get reprints close to the original. The workflow includes scan calibration, test prints on related media, and file adjustments based on what comes off the printer. We can't guarantee a perfect match, since pigment ink printers have limits (more on that below) and any printer or media swap downstream changes the result. What we can do is get visibly close, on our own equipment.",
  },
  {
    title: "Print-ready files, your formats",
    body:
      "TIFF or JPEG at the resolution your reprint needs. RGB or sRGB on request. We hand the files over and they're yours, we don't watermark, retain rights, or repurpose your work.",
  },
];

const useCases = [
  "Original paintings being turned into a print run",
  "Illustrators digitising work for licensing or print-on-demand",
  "Galleries and dealers cataloguing inventory",
  "Estates digitising a deceased artist's body of work",
  "Insurance archives that need accurate visual records",
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What format do you deliver, and at what resolution?",
    a: `TIFF or JPEG at 300 PPI relative to the original size as standard, higher on request. We can also export specifically sized for a target print (e.g. "300 PPI at 60 × 80 cm"). RGB by default; we can convert to sRGB or a specific ICC profile if your printer needs one.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Do I retain copyright and ownership of the files?",
    a: `Yes, completely. We're providing a digitisation service. The files are yours from the moment we deliver them. We don't watermark, register, license, or reuse your work in any way.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What's colour matching, and is it always needed?",
    a: `Colour matching is our best-effort process for getting a reprint as close to the original as we can. We do scan calibration, run test prints on related media, and adjust the file until the printed result reads as close to the source as practical. It can be a time intensive process depending on the image and this coupled with having to run prints on real media is why we charge $150.\n\nImportant honest caveat: we can't guarantee a perfect match. Even with careful work the result depends on the printer, the inks, and the substrate, and not every colour in the original is reproducible (see the next question).\n\nYou also need to be mindful that colour corrected prints may not look "right" on your monitor simply because your monitor is not calibrated or even capable of seeing all the different colours in a file. There is also times where certain colours need to be fundamentally altered to show up in a print, but can make the digital file feel "off" even if the print output is a match for the original.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Can you reproduce every colour in my artwork?",
    a: `Not always, no. We print using high-end pigment ink printers from Canon. These sort of machines are specially designed for fine art printing and they produce exceptional results and details, but they have limitations too. Some colours may fall outside of what is possible to print. Some colours may even fall outside of what image editing software can reproduce. In these instances software and printers choose the "closest alternative" and this sometimes is good enough and sometimes requires further adjustments.\n\nOne area that pigment ink machines are not great at capturing is really fluorescent colours. In these situations we can capture those sorts of colours but we probably can't reproduce them on our printers. You could probably take your files though and work with a UV printer to produce those fluorescent tones.`,
  },
  {
    q: "If I take your file to another printer, will the colour still match?",
    a: `Its unlikely to be a perfect match. Colour matching is tied to the printer, the ink, and the media being used. We tune your files to look right on our equipment and with the media we stock. A different printer may not even bother with colour management or they may simply have vastly different equipment and media. The results could therefore differ substantially.\n\nIf you plan to print elsewhere, just let us know upfront and we can probably share with you a PSD file with the adjustment layers included.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What's the largest size you can scan?",
    a: `Theres not really a size limitation as such but our flatbed scanner does max out at A3 size. This is unfortunately a common limitation with high resolution scanners, so if that sounds small to you, we agree. What we usually do beyond A3 size is create multiple scans of the item and then stitch them together in Photoshop. It works very effectively and enables us to scan and handle much bigger works without having to go down the route of photography.\n\nGenerally we try not to photograph, though we do have a proper studio set-up when required. In our experience the flatbed scanning route provides the sharpest and most accurate output and also enables us to upscale better if you want to increase the size of the original in the future.`,
  },
  {
    q: "How long does it take?",
    a: `Single-piece A3 with colour matching: typically 3 to 5 working days. Larger pieces or batches take longer because of the stitching and review steps. We confirm a turnaround when we quote.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Can you also print from the file you make?",
    a: `Yes. Many clients keep the workflow under one roof: we scan, you approve the file, and we print on archival paper or canvas. Same colour pipeline end to end, so the printed result matches what we delivered digitally.`,
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Art scanning and digitisation in Singapore | Fine Art Printing"
  description={description}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 pt-14 pb-12 md:pt-20 md:pb-20">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:items-center md:gap-14">
        <div class="md:col-span-6">
          <Eyebrow>Art scanning</Eyebrow>
          <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-[clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3rem)] font-medium leading-[1.08] text-ink text-balance">
            Studio-grade artwork scanning, with best-effort colour matching.
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-[640px] text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
            High-resolution digitisation of original artwork for reprints,
            licensing, and archival records. Flatbed-first workflow with
            optional colour matching to get reprints as close to the original
            as our equipment can manage. Some colours, like fluorescents,
            sit outside what pigment-ink printers can reproduce, we'll flag
            those upfront.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
            <a
              href="#quote"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-pill bg-petrol px-5 py-2.5 text-[14px] font-semibold text-white transition-colors hover:bg-[#142a40]"
            >
              Get a price now <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
            </a>
            <a
              href={waLink}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-pill border border-[#1ebe5b] bg-[#25D366] px-5 py-2.5 text-[14px] font-semibold text-white transition-colors hover:bg-[#1ebe5b]"
            >
              <WhatsAppIcon size={16} />
              <span>WhatsApp us</span>
            </a>
          </div>

          <GoogleReviewsBadge class="mt-6" />
        </div>

        <div class="md:col-span-6">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/photos/german-etching-1.jpg"
              alt="Studio-grade scan of a fine art original being prepared for print reproduction"
              class="aspect-[4/5] w-full rounded-card object-cover shadow-[0_24px_60px_-24px_rgba(0,0,0,0.35)]"
            />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why scan with us -->
  <section class="border-t border-border-soft bg-[#FAF7F1]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[680px] text-center">
        <Eyebrow>Why scan with us</Eyebrow>
        <DisplayHeading level={2} class="mt-3">
          Built for reproduction, not capture.
        </DisplayHeading>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          We scan artwork the way printers want to receive it. Flat, sharp,
          and tuned for colour as carefully as we can manage, in a format
          that gives your reprint the best chance of looking like the
          original.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="mt-14 grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-10 gap-y-12 sm:grid-cols-3">
        {whyScanWithUs.map((row, i) => (
          <li class="border-t border-ink/10 pt-5">
            <span class="font-serif italic text-[14px] text-[var(--color-gold-text)]">
              {String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <h3 class="mt-2 font-serif text-[20px] font-medium leading-[1.3] text-ink">
              {row.title}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
              {row.body}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quoter -->
  <section id="quote" class="border-t border-border-soft bg-white scroll-mt-6">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="md:col-span-4">
          <Eyebrow>Estimate the cost</Eyebrow>
          <DisplayHeading level={2} class="mt-3">
            Get a price now.
          </DisplayHeading>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-[380px] text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
            Pick the size of your original and whether you need calibrated
            colour matching. Submit photos for an exact quote, condition and
            surface texture can shift the workflow.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-[380px] text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
            Restoring an old photo instead?{" "}
            <a href="/scanning-restoration" class="text-petrol underline-offset-2 hover:underline">
              See photo restoration →
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md:col-span-8">
          <PhotoServiceCalculator fixedKind="art-scanning" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Use cases -->
  <section class="border-t border-border-soft bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[680px] text-center">
        <Eyebrow>Who it's for</Eyebrow>
        <DisplayHeading level={2} class="mt-3">
          Common use cases.
        </DisplayHeading>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          The output we produce works for any flow that ends in a printed
          reproduction or a licensable digital asset.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-12 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-2">
        {useCases.map((u) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-card border border-border-soft bg-white p-4">
            <span class="mt-0.5 inline-flex h-5 w-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-petrol/10 text-petrol">
              <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M2 6l3 3 5-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
              </svg>
            </span>
            <span class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink">{u}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pair with print -->
  <section class="border-t border-border-soft bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-[820px] text-center">
        <Eyebrow>What's next</Eyebrow>
        <DisplayHeading level={2} class="mt-3">
          Print from your scan.
        </DisplayHeading>
        <p class="mt-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          Once you've approved the digital file, we can run reprints with
          the same colour pipeline that produced the scan. Same studio, no
          handover, the print matches the source.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
        <a
          href="/print-on-paper"
          class="group block rounded-card border border-border-soft bg-white p-6 transition-shadow hover:shadow-[0_12px_32px_-12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.18)]"
        >
          <p class="font-serif text-[20px] font-medium text-ink">Print on paper</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">Archival Hahnemühle papers, configured to your size.</p>
          <span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[13px] font-semibold text-petrol">
            View paper options
            <span class="transition-transform duration-150 group-hover:translate-x-0.5" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </span>
        </a>
        <a
          href="/print-on-canvas"
          class="group block rounded-card border border-border-soft bg-white p-6 transition-shadow hover:shadow-[0_12px_32px_-12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.18)]"
        >
          <p class="font-serif text-[20px] font-medium text-ink">Print on canvas</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">Premium canvas substrates, stretched and framed in-house.</p>
          <span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[13px] font-semibold text-petrol">
            View canvas options
            <span class="transition-transform duration-150 group-hover:translate-x-0.5" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="border-t border-border-soft bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="md:col-span-4">
          <Eyebrow>FAQ</Eyebrow>
          <DisplayHeading level={2} class="mt-3">
            Common questions.
          </DisplayHeading>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-[360px] text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
            If yours isn't here, send us a photo and dimensions, we'll come
            back with a steer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="md:col-span-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4">
          {faqs.map(({ q, a }) => (
            <li class="rounded-card border border-border-soft bg-white p-6">
              <h3 class="font-serif text-[18px] font-medium leading-[1.3] text-ink">
                {q}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-subtle whitespace-pre-line">
                {a}
              </p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <DarkCTA />
</BaseLayout>
